#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""duo2-autosleep: sleep like a phone.

When both panels are dark (duo-wc's event stream: nothing lit, no peek or
glance), wait DELAY seconds, then suspend (s2idle, through logind, so its
delay inhibitors and the system-sleep hooks run) unless something holds the
phone awake:

  - the maintenance file /etc/duo2/autosleep-hold exists (the switch: while it
    is there nothing is ever suspended),
  - a timed hold has not lapsed yet (/etc/duo2/autosleep-hold-until: an end
    time, written by `duo2-autosleep-switch hold 2h` for remote work; it
    survives a reboot, lapses by itself, and an end more than HOLD_MAX_S
    ahead is not trusted: a clock that was wrong when it was written),
  - external power is connected (a charger, a computer's USB port) and
    STAY_AWAKE_CHARGING is on (the default): the panels still go dark on
    their timeout, the phone stays reachable over the network. Not at or
    below CHARGING_HOLD_FLOOR % while the battery is not charging (a source
    too weak for the awake phone: asleep it charges). This setting is read
    again whenever autosleep.conf changes (the switch's `charging on|off`,
    Duo Settings), not only at start. A --test-max run leaves it out
    unless --charging-hold on says otherwise (a test suspend is asked for),
  - a call is dialling, ringing or active (ModemManager),
  - audio plays or records (an ALSA PCM is running) or an MPRIS player says
    Playing,
  - a logind "sleep" inhibitor in block mode (systemd-inhibit, PackageKit, ...),
  - a USB host has the phone's gadget configured,
  - an ssh session of one of SSH_USERS is open,
  - a Clocks alarm rings within the next DELAY + ALARM_LEAD seconds,
  - the torch is lit (the camera's flash LED as a light, the quick menu's
    Flashlight): it stays lit with the panels dark, as on Android, and a
    suspend would switch it off (the system-sleep hook duo2-torch-off: the
    kernel leaves it as it was, and the flash module's supply in s2idle is
    not something to rely on).

Dark is duo-wc's word: a panel lit for any reason is not dark, so an app
that keeps the screen on (duo-wc's idle inhibit: a video, a map; its state's
`idle_inhibit`) keeps the phone awake too, through the lit panels and
nothing else. With the panels dark on purpose (the power key) an idle
inhibitor holds nothing here: a video's sound does (the ALSA PCM), and an
app that must run on with the panels dark (navigation with voice
guidance) takes a logind sleep inhibitor (`systemd-inhibit --what=sleep
--mode=block APP`, or its own Inhibit call to logind; duo2-android-location
takes one for an Android app that uses the GPS).

Before it suspends it arms a CLOCK_REALTIME_ALARM timer (the kernel's
alarmtimer, on the PMIC RTC rtc1) ALARM_LEAD seconds before the next Clocks
alarm (gnome-clocks rings only while it runs, and only after the phone is
awake), and WAKE_EVERY seconds ahead if set. systemd timers with
WakeSystem=true program the same RTC themselves. The other wake sources are
the kernel's: the power key, the RTC, the modem, the WiFi (WoWLAN, if set).

After a resume it stays awake until WiFi answers again (at most WIFI_WAIT
seconds) and RESUME_GRACE seconds after that (longer up to a Clocks alarm it
woke for), then sleeps again while the panels stay dark. On a duo2-try boot
(duo2.try= on the command line) it never suspends by itself: a try kernel
must stay reachable. It
logs every suspend and resume: how long it slept, what fired (the wake
interrupts that moved), when WiFi answered again and when a panel lit.

WiFi in sleep (WIFI_SLEEP). "off": the WLAN chip is powered off in every
sleep and WiFi reconnects after the resume (~4.5 s); nothing that arrives
over WiFi wakes the phone, so with WIFI_SYNC_EVERY set it wakes that often
on WiFi to let the push connections deliver (SYNC_GRACE seconds from WiFi's
answer, then SYNC_HOOK, a command that pokes them). "wowlan": the chip stays
associated (WoWLAN) and wakes the phone for what WOWLAN_WAKE lets through
(tcp: TCP to the phone's own addresses, the push connections; unicast: any
frame to its MAC; ping: echo requests to it; magic: a Wake-on-LAN packet;
disconnect: the access point drops it); after such a wake it stays up
WIFI_GRACE seconds. Before every suspend the patterns are made from the
addresses wlan0 has then (nothing is logged of them), and the Root Port's
power/wakeup is set to match (it arms the chip's WAKE# where the DT has it).
WoWLAN needs a kernel that keeps the PCIe link up for a wakeup and lets the
MSIs wake; WOWLAN_MAX_FAILURES sleeps in a row after which WiFi did not
answer within WIFI_WAIT switch it off until the daemon restarts. The kernel
command line overrides any setting for one boot: duo2.autosleep.KEY=VALUE
(a duo2-try of WoWLAN without touching autosleep.conf), /run/duo2/autosleep.conf
(same format, read after autosleep.conf at start) until the next boot, and
duo2.autosleep.on_try=1 lets a try boot sleep (a measurement series; its
netwatch then needs duo2.netwatch.loss= longer than a sleep).

An empty battery powers the phone off cleanly (a phone that runs dry in
suspend loses whatever was not on disk). On battery power at or below
POWEROFF_LEVEL percent it powers off: at once with the panels dark, after a
notification and POWEROFF_NOTICE seconds with a panel lit (a charger plugged
in meanwhile calls it off), after the call during a call (down to 1 %); a
lit torch is switched off first (duo2-torch-off.service does it at every
shutdown too, but not when systemctl poweroff has to be --force). The
level is read every minute awake, right before every suspend and right
after every resume; and every suspend on battery is bounded by an RTC wake
to look again: half the time the margin above POWEROFF_LEVEL lasts at
SLEEP_DRAIN_MA (or at the drain measured in the last long sleep, if more):
~40 min at 15 %, ~7 min at 5 %. Such a battery check wakes for a moment
(REQUEST_GRACE, no WiFi wait). The hold file and a try boot do not stop
this: it is a safety net, not automatic sleep.

  duo2-autosleep [--dry-run] [--test-max S] [--once] [--no-ssh-inhibit]
                 [--delay S] [--resume-grace S] [--poweroff-test LEVEL]
                 [--wifi-sleep off|wowlan] [--wowlan-wake LIST]
                 [--charging-hold on|off]

--dry-run decides as usual but logs "would suspend" instead. --test-max S
(1-120) bounds every suspend with an RTC wake alarm S seconds ahead, set with
rtcwake before the suspend (a test must always come back by itself).
--once exits after the first resume (always so with --test-max: a test is
one suspend; a --test-max or --dry-run run also ends after 300 s of wall
clock). --no-ssh-inhibit ignores ssh sessions
(a test started over ssh); --ignore-hold ignores the hold file (only with
--test-max or --dry-run). --poweroff-test LEVEL never suspends and does only
the empty-battery part, with LEVEL as the threshold (a test of the
notification and the power-off on a charged phone, for 300 s of wall clock;
with --dry-run it logs "would power off" instead). Without it, --test-max
and --dry-run only log what the battery would make them do. --wifi-sleep and
--wowlan-wake override the settings for this run; a --test-max run that set
WoWLAN up switches it off again before it ends. --charging-hold sets
STAY_AWAKE_CHARGING for this run. --ignore-hold ignores the hold file only:
a timed hold holds a test run too (another agent may be working on the
phone).

Settings: /etc/duo2/autosleep.conf (key = value; see autosleep.conf).
"""

